can also be expressed simply in polar coordinates.\n\nSo, let's transform the entire problem to polar coordinates using the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ defined by\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its preimage $\\Delta$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9901b20d218bef2935dba64612495874d08388dc6d7ae21f8a1de596296bc276.png)\n\n             \n\nTo express our integral in terms of polar coordinates, we will use the **change of variables formula,** given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, {{\\color{black}{\\mathrm d A}}} = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  {{\\color{black}{r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta}}}.\n$$\n\nWhen expressing our integral in polar coordinates, we must write \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}{\\textrm d A}} = {\\color{black}{r\\,\\textrm d r\\textrm d \\theta}}.\n$$\n\nIt's important not to forget the extra factor of $r.$\n\nNow, applying the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\left( {x^2 + y^2} \\right)\\,\\mathrm d A}} = 8\\pi.\n$$\n\nIn terms of transformations, the change of variables formula maps the non-rectangular integration domain $D$ to the rectangular domain $\\Delta,$ making the integral much easier to evaluate." 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186130404 1064 "Evaluating double integrals by changing the variables to polar coordinates is often easier than using Cartesian coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\left( {x^2 + y^2} \\right)\\,\\mathrm d A}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is the disk with center $(0,0)$ and radius $2,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/94/945f3881b5348fffb922d7b57fb42a6262eb03861fb5aa2411b947a16dcb3b59.png)\n\nThis is a challenging integral to evaluate as it currently stands. However, we should recognize that the problem naturally lends itself to plane polar coordinates because\n\n- the domain $D$ is a disc of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ which has a simple polar representation, and\n\n- the integrand $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ can also be expressed simply in polar coordinates.\n\nSo, let's transform the entire problem to polar coordinates using the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ defined by\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its preimage $\\Delta$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9901b20d218bef2935dba64612495874d08388dc6d7ae21f8a1de596296bc276.png)\n\n             \n\nTo express our integral in terms of polar coordinates, we will use the **change of variables formula,** given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, {{\\color{black}{\\mathrm d A}}} = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  {{\\color{black}{r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta}}}.\n$$\n\nWhen expressing our integral in polar coordinates, we must write \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}{\\textrm d A}} = {\\color{black}{r\\,\\textrm d r\\textrm d \\theta}}.\n$$\n\nIt's important not to forget the extra factor of $r.$\n\nNow, applying the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{black}\\mathrm{d}A} & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{black}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta } \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\left( {x^2 + y^2} \\right)\\,\\mathrm d A}} = 8\\pi.\n$$\n\nIn terms of transformations, the change of variables formula maps the non-rectangular integration domain $D$ to the rectangular domain $\\Delta,$ making the integral much easier to evaluate." 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186130406 1064 "Let's get a little more practice at writing regions in polar coordinates.\n\nConsider the semicircular disc of radius $1$ centered $(1,0)$ in the first quadrant, shown below. The equation of the curve is $y=\\sqrt{2x-x^2}.$\n\n![](images/0f/0f4aec039a0de31d8f5ea24dc9db8c83f236dfd66e9f181768dae45ab0fd6b07.png)\n\nFirst, we recall that the following equations relate Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's now use the above formulas to write the function $y=\\sqrt{2x-x^2}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = 2\\cos\\theta.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=2\\cos\\theta$ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/35/3584d2f0d9ee2c0ce469bfc117c67c1efd1f4fd08c96c7beef00198624977d06.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/2c/2c5731478941621db03e8949eac217dcdfc604b3efd0312b56c59b9874d6be98.png)\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2\\cos\\theta, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186130406 1064 "Let's get a little more practice at writing regions in polar coordinates.\n\nConsider the semicircular disc of radius $1$ centered $(1,0)$ in the first quadrant, shown below. The equation of the curve is $y=\\sqrt{2x-x^2}.$\n\n![](images/0f/0f4aec039a0de31d8f5ea24dc9db8c83f236dfd66e9f181768dae45ab0fd6b07.png)\n\nFirst, we recall that the following equations relate Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's now use the above formulas to write the function $y=\\sqrt{2x-x^2}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = 2\\cos\\theta.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=2\\cos\\theta$ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/35/3584d2f0d9ee2c0ce469bfc117c67c1efd1f4fd08c96c7beef00198624977d06.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/2c/2c5731478941621db03e8949eac217dcdfc604b3efd0312b56c59b9874d6be98.png)\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2\\cos\\theta, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186130456 1064 "We can evaluate triple integrals by changing the variables to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the finite region enclosed between the surfaces $z =0$ and $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a63e349d2776fe07077379b9d603e32ad2ff264cfa8005a226422b44f7e100fe.png)\n\n \n\nThis is a challenging integral to evaluate as it currently stands. However, we should recognize that the problem naturally lends itself to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nRecall that if $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b2/b2f8a6fe7f21b062eec294522e411c1e471b564eae68763d4767f917d65b54b0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $R$ is\n\n- bounded below by $z = 0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2 = 1 - r^2.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1-r^2 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/da/dae76df462a695e655ffdde01559d19bf245aa6d601100fb8c7bc1024106a4c8.png)\n\n \n\nTo express our integral in terms of cylindrical polar coordinates, we will use the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, {\\color{blue}\\mathrm{d}V} = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nWhen expressing our integral in cylindrical polar coordinates, we must write \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{d}V}} = {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nIt's important not to forget the extra factor of ${\\color{blue}{r}}.$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1}} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1 - r^2 }} \\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black} r^3} \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}r \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n=\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186130456 1064 "We can evaluate triple integrals by changing the variables to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the finite region enclosed between the surfaces $z =0$ and $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a63e349d2776fe07077379b9d603e32ad2ff264cfa8005a226422b44f7e100fe.png)\n\n \n\nThis is a challenging integral to evaluate as it currently stands. However, we should recognize that the problem naturally lends itself to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nRecall that if $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b2/b2f8a6fe7f21b062eec294522e411c1e471b564eae68763d4767f917d65b54b0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $R$ is\n\n- bounded below by $z = 0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2 = 1 - r^2.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1-r^2 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/da/dae76df462a695e655ffdde01559d19bf245aa6d601100fb8c7bc1024106a4c8.png)\n\n \n\nTo express our integral in terms of cylindrical polar coordinates, we will use the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, {\\color{blue}\\mathrm{d}V} = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nWhen expressing our integral in cylindrical polar coordinates, we must write \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{d}V}} = {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nIt's important not to forget the extra factor of ${\\color{blue}{r}}.$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[white, 2pt]{{\\color{black}1}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[white, 2pt]{{\\color{black}1-{r}^{2}}}}\\bbox[white, 2pt]{{\\color{black}{r}^{3}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1}} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1 - r^2 }} \\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black} r^3} \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}r \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n=\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186130552 1078 #edb/ref 17592186130553 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186130720 1078 #edb/ref 17592186130723 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186130756 1078 #edb/ref 17592186130759 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] 