i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186134376 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}2t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{{u}^{3}|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186134376 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, 