 function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186167050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163378 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186167050 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=(x-a){e}^{x+y}\\,i+x{e}^{x+y}\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186167050 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(x-a){e}^{x+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x{e}^{x+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186167050 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=(x-a){e}^{x+y},\\,Q=x{e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=(x-a){e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=(x+1){e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a){e}^{x+y} & =(x+1){e}^{x+y} \\\\ (x-a){e}^{x+y}-(x+1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ (x-a-x-1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ -(a+1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-1.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186167050 1074 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ the domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=(x-a){e}^{x+y},\\,Q=x{e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=(x-a){e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=(x+1){e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a){e}^{x+y} & =(x+1){e}^{x+y} \\\\ (x-a){e}^{x+y}-(x+1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ (x-a-x-1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ -(a+1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-1.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186167090 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=2ay\\,i+(3-x)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186167090 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=2ay\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186167090 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=2ay,\\,Q=3-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2a,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a & =-1 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186167090 1074 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ the domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=2ay,\\,Q=3-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2a,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a & =-1 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186167541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166454 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186167541 1074 "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186167541 1074 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{x}^{2}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=2xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{x}^{2}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186168082 1064 "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is **conservative** if there exists a scalar function $f$ such that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla \\! f.\n$$\n\nIf such a function $f$ exists, it is called a **potential function** for $\\mathbf F.$\n\nFor example, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$ is a potential function for $\\mathbf F$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNot all vector fields are conservative. However, conservative vector fields have many interesting and useful properties, as we'll soon discover." 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186168082 1064 "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is **conservative** if there exists a scalar function $f$ such that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla \\! f.\n$$\n\nIf such a function $f$ exists, it is called a **potential function** for $\\mathbf F.$\n\nFor example, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$ is a potential function for $\\mathbf F$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\  & =F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNot all vector fields are conservative. However, conservative vector fields have many interesting and useful properties, as we'll soon discover." 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186172280 1071 "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186172280 1071 "Determine the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186172280 1074 "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ne \\pm x}\\,.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186172280 1074 "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{