the origin removed:\n\n![](images/5b/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png)" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186172281 1074 "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/e5/e54618f7e72d70f299686ca2846a1d6fa0caccb8daecb090b3a653271c88fcd3.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](images/5b/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png)" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186172301 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\,i+2xy{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186172301 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186172301 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2).$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y{e}^{x{y}^{2}}(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}2,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}2\\cdot {2}^{2}}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}2}-1 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}{2}^{4}}-1 \\\\  & ={2}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186172301 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y{e}^{x{y}^{2}}(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{2}}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{4}}-1 \\\\  & ={2}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186172302 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=4x-3y.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(1,-3)$ in ${R}^{2}.$"