}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186180029 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186180029 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186180030 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+5\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186180030 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186180030 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $5,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(5)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5{cos}^{2}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={5(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =5(0+\\pi -1)-5(0-1) \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186180030 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $5,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}t)-(5)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+5\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(2t)+1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={5(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(2t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =5(0+\\pi -1)-5(0-1) \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186180038 1071 "![](images/c7/c71395cfe5877546509f246