x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186235535 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2x+0 \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186235537 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe required rate is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                        