\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{e}^{x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y). \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point  $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186259485 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}y) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y-{e}^{x}(-\\operatorname{sin}y). \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point  $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F})(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186259486 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡yz,\\,xyz,2⟩.$ Find $\\text{div}\\,F.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186259486 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=⟨{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}yz,\\hspace{0.167em}xyz,2⟩.$ Find $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186259486 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡yz+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186259486 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}yz+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186259487 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=(2x+3y-4z)^{2}\\,i+(2x+3y-4z)^{2}\\,j+(2x+3y-4z)^{2}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186259487 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(2x+3y-4z)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2x+3y-4z)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2x+3y-4z)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ \nFind $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186259487 1074 "If $F=P\\,+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{a