{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(4)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}\\right)-(2)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}-1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{t}-t){|}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =(1-2)-(4-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186262735 1071 "![](images/85/85b4da8c0fa35867871b6254ba20f505b7596f2c97226adb2ed01339fe4cbedf.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=5{e}^{-x}\\,i+4\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,e],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186262735 1071 "![](images/85/85b4da8c0fa35867871b6254ba20f505b7596f2c97226adb2ed01339fe4cbedf.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=5{e}^{-x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ through the curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [1,e],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186262735 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/55/559bbc20aa7d47eaad47a8975f80c8efb91bd03bc816357bc6047675dc755c9b.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$