(1)-(2{t}^{2})\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}){|}_{1/e}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186262741 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [\\frac{1}{e},1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(1,\\frac{1}{e})$ to the point $(0,1).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{n}$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf{T},$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/08/08fc4561a322b690da7bd28a9e69a91c687256e8a902ec7690dda5e6a1c689a6.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =0, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2{y}^{2}=2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=-\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}(0)\\cdot (1)-(2{t}^{2})\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/e}^{1}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}){|}_{1/e}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186262752 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2x,\\,{x}^{5}-3y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186262752 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}y+2x,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{5}-3y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186262752 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/35/35e378bca35f4b0822c713556065415d59b5c566ec8fcc8e3124256aac7b7f14.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{5}-3y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q