 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+y{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+2)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1-xz) \\\\  & =3+x+(-x) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186281500 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+y{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+2)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1-xz) \\\\  & =3+x+(-x) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}3\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186281501 1071 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented su