ace that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+2xy+2xy \\\\  & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}4xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}4xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186282710 1080 "{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+\\operatorname{cos}s\\operatorname{cos}t" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186282710 1080 "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186283327 1080 "{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+\\operatorname{cos}s\\operatorname{sin}t" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186283327 1080 "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186283689 1080 "{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+\\operatorname{sin}s\\operatorname{cos}t" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186283689 1080 "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186284668 1074 "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t(-\\operatorname{sin}t)+(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}s)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+\\operatorname{sin}s\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186284668 1074 "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186285245 1080 "-4\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186285245 1080 "-4i-6j-4k" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186285284 1080 "6\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}-6\\mathbf{k}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186285284 1080 "6i-4j-6k" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186285293 1080 "8\\mathbf{i}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186285293 1080 "8i" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186285377 1080 "-14\\mathbf{i}+18\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186285377 1080 "-14i+18j+6k" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186285479 1080 "8\\mathbf{i}+12\\mathbf{j}-7\\mathbf{k}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186285479 1080 "8i+12j-7k" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186285893 1080 "6\\mathbf{j}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186285893 1080 "6j" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186285913 1080 "3\\mathbf{k}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186285913 1080 "3k" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186285977 1080 "8\\mathbf{i}+12\\mathbf{j}-17\\mathbf{k}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186285977 1080 "8i+12j-17k" 1319