cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}+(-3{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186362533 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}+(-3{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186362536 1071 "The scalar curl of $F$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186362536 1071 "The scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186362536 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{3})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0-(3x\\cdot 2y) \\\\  & =-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186362536 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{3})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0-(3x\\cdot 2y) \\\\  & =-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186362542 1074 "Notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}-6(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186362542 1074 "Notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}-6(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186362552 1071 "![](images/cf/cfbc570ba4fc6bd4923edc2d0efae0e735e671f9b4350226159e591e0bdae6c7.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nThe net $L$ is represented by the line segment shown in the diagram above. \nCalculate the flux of $F$ through $L,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{L}^{}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\\,=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186362552 1071 "![](images/cf/cfbc570ba4fc6bd4923edc2d0efae0e735e671f9b4350226159e591e0bdae6c7.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nThe net $L$ is represented by the line segment shown in the diagram above. \nCalculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $L,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n{\\int }_{L}^{\\phantom{C}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s\\hspace{0.167em}=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186362552 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n* the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n* the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the line segment $L,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}t,\\,y=-\\sqrt{2}t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the line from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3x{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}t)(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{2} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-{y}^{3} \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}t)=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{2}t)=-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{L}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{L}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2})-(2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-12{t}^{3}-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-16{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4[{t}^{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4(1-0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186362552 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n* the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n* the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nParameterizing the line segment $L,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}t,\\,y=-\\sqrt{2}t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the line from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{n}$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf{T},$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3x{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}t)(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{2} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-{y}^{3} \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}t)=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{2}t)=-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{L}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{L}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2})-(2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-12{t}^{3}-4{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-16{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4[{t}^{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4(1-0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186362555 1071 "![](images/83/8354a964dc235f1c9676754cf7d163cb4a54f05bd5899ff398acb11a4287861d.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nLet $∂R$ be the boundary of the region $R,$ shown above. Use Green's theorem to calculate the following integral:\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂R}^{}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\,={∮}_{∂R}^{}3x{y}^{2}\\,dx-{y}^{3}\\,dy\\,=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186362555 1071 "![](images/83/8354a964dc235f1c9676754cf7d163cb4a54f05bd5899ff398acb11a4287861d.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nLet $∂R$ be the boundary of the region $R,$ shown above. Use Green's theorem to calculate the following integral:\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n{∮}_{∂R}^{\\phantom{C}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\\hspace{0.167em}={∮}_{∂R}^{\\phantom{C}}3x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x-{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186362555 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nAs we saw earlier, we can use Green's theorem to write our line integral in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{∂R}3x{y}^{2}\\,dx-{y}^{3}\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nBut this time, our region $R$ (in polar coordinates) is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3{[\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2}]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{r=1}^{r=2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186362555 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nAs we saw earlier, we can use Green's theorem to write our line integral in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{∂R}3x{y}^{2}\\,dx-{y}^{3}\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nBut this time, our region $R$ (in polar coordinates) is given by \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3{[\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2}]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{r=1}^{r=2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186365555 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨1,-3,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨4,2,-1⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186365555 1071 "Let $u=⟨1,-3,2⟩$ and $v=⟨4,2,-1⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186365555 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-3)(-1)-(2)(2))\\mathbf{i}-((1)(-1)-(2)(4))\\mathbf{j}+((1)(2)-(-3)(4))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3-4)\\mathbf{i}-(-1-8)\\mathbf{j}+(2+12)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-1\\mathbf{i}+9\\mathbf{j}+14\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,9,14⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186365555 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-3)(-1)-(2)(2))i-((1)(-1)-(2)(4))j+((1)(2)-(-3)(4))k \\\\  & =(3-4)i-(-1-8)j+(2+12)k \\\\  & =-1i+9j+14k \\\\  & =⟨-1,9,14⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186366191 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨5,\\sqrt{2},0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,1,0⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186366191 1071 "Let $a=⟨5,\\sqrt{2},0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,1,0⟩.$ Find $a\\times b.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186366191 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}5 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(5\\cdot 0-0\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(5\\cdot 1-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\mathbf{i}-0\\cdot \\mathbf{j}+(5+\\sqrt{2})\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,0,5+\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186366191 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}5 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1)i-(5\\cdot 0-0\\cdot (-1))j+(5\\cdot 1-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =0\\cdot i-0\\cdot j+(5+\\sqrt{2})k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,5+\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186366198 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨4,2,-1⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨x,2,-1⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{20}.$ Find $x.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186366198 1071 "Let $a=⟨4,2,-1⟩$, $b=⟨x,2,-1⟩$ and $|\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{20}.$ Find $x.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186366198 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begi