tations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/2b/2bed1b367cd101e0e90674c619ad87da8c90744df570eeea4d9b0333ccee0de1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ and the inner curve ${C}_{3}$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =-5+(-2)-(-9) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186369504 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{4}y-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})^{2}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot ({3}^{6}-{1}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{728\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186369504 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{4}y-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}