186576411 false] [17592186373979 1071 "For a surface $S$ given by $y=h(x,z)$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}+Q-R\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $\\mathbf{j}$-component.*" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186373979 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=h(x,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,z)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}h(x,z),\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'} & =⟨0,\\frac{∂h}{∂z},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{∂h}{∂x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{∂h}{∂z} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $\\mathbf{j}$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}=-{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'}=⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,z))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({\\color{blue}-P}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}\\frac{∂h}{∂x}}+Q-R\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}\\frac{∂h}{∂z}})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186373979 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=h(x,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,h(x,z),\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\frac{∂h}{∂z},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{∂h}{∂x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{∂h}{∂z} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,-1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,1,\\,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={