gned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186377082 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186377088 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{4}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =2{\\left(-\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{16}-1 \\\\ y & =3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [1759218637708