2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2},$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/bd/bdf02ff330f256327d0c9f1147c388b25184610b36ea7ab77bb2c570ec3be19a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/0d/0d3180a60e33c05e904c8fc00fdec4197510198fef3de3fa8c7b3549609205f6.png)" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186382682 1074 "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186382682 1074 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+1))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+1))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186382684 1080 "\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186382684 1080 "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186382685 1080 "\\frac{xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,i-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,j" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186382685 1080 "\\frac{xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186382686 1080 "\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,j" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186382686 1080 "\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186382687 1080 "\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186382687 1080 "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i-\\frac{xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186382688 1080 "\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186382688 1080 "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186382689 1074 "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n