{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186387222 1071 "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-y}\\,j\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186387222 1071 "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186387222 1074 "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-y}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<3.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $\\sqrt{3},$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/f9/f9c3a22a56839165bfd6c3b05f78ca25053553532063192836d0332d2b070436.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-y=0\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the line $y=x$ removed:\n\n![](images/a1/a1ef93cc90bdd59b922723c2c2aa2bd670b43e416923892c9db53b36ac6b7e57.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f70cab5ec58e63e1751faa65b575a20da748b3e8ee90cffac8f1f3b029bbbd79.png)" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186387222 1074 "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-y}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<3.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $\\sqrt{3},$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/f9/f9c3a22a56839165bfd6c3b05f78ca25053553532063192836d0332d2b070436.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-y=0\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the entire plane ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with the line $y=x$ removed:\n\n![](images/a1/a1ef93cc90bdd59b922723c2c2aa2bd670b43e416923892c9db53b36ac6b7e57.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f70cab5ec58e63e1751faa65b575a20da748b3e8ee90cffac8f1f3b029bbbd79.png)" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186387685 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/9e/9eceb8ab173995a8325fb1a8452baaa7f1071e569391e9a49b69c047d76f3b52.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186387685 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/9e/9eceb8ab173995a8325fb1a8452baaa7f1071e569391e9a49b69c047d76f3b52.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\hspace{0.222em}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186387699 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d8ee02b2b7282349604dc6305c0b2be796cfdbd2937e06d089e660683a5785ad.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(2xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 5,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\