ligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186393639 1074 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t).$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}(t)⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le 2e\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}(t))\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{e}^{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}t}=2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2e}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2e}2{t}^{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186393639 1074 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t).$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2e\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\