369 17592186576411 true] [17592186408112 1074 "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\,(3\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 5)\\,-\\,2\\,(0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)\\,+\\,4\\,(0\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\,(-5)\\,-\\,2\\,(-2)\\,+\\,4\\,(-6) \\\\  & =5+4-24 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186409609 1071 "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,\n$$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186409609 1071 "Given that $$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$ what is the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}?$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186409609 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186409609 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl