 the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y) \\\\  & =2x+xz+0 \\\\  & =2x+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(2x+xz)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2x+xz}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186431049 1071 "![](images/a9/a95b161dab89fe1245724921ef4ee36112d7a4062e7e171411e4ffe61253b91f.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}t-\\operatorname{sin}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{cos}2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186431049 1071 "![](images/a9/a95b161dab89fe1245724921ef4ee36112d7a4062e7e171411e4ffe61253b91f.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186431049 1074 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t-2\\operatorname{cos}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\operatorname{sin}t+2\\operatorname{sin}2t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186431049 1074 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integra