h a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186442252 1071 "![](images/0a/0a60729b626e1e9e219a839898cacc18aa81ec2a9b1b445e7b92b721874c2550.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186442252 1071 "![](images/0a/0a60729b626e1e9e219a839898cacc18aa81ec2a9b1b445e7b92b721874c2550.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area 