 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨x+\\sqrt{y},\\,y+\\sqrt{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the line $y=0$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186481405 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨x+\\sqrt{y},\\hspace{0.222em}y+\\sqrt{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the line $y=0$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186481405 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e570374b37c116e32c3a7762b8cf09a28bdbb3e06b5e67e25837902a89bcd264.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y+\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186481405 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e570374b37c116e32c3a7762b8cf09a28bdbb3e06b5e67e25837902a89bcd264.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y+\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}x\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186483360 1071 "![](images/2b/2b64c156519b685acf390c51c1ea3e1540d82e0f7e047cf2f804d38a23956604.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186483360 1071 "![](images/2b/2b64c156519b685acf390c51c1ea3e1540d82e0f7e047cf2f804d38a23956604.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf{F}?$\n\nI. $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=0$\n\nII. $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}>0$\n\nIII. $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}<0$\n\nIV. $\\mathbf{F}$ is solenoidal" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186483360 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the length of the vectors' $x$-components increase as we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186483360 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=0$ everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf{F},$ we say that $\\mathbf{F}$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the length of the vectors' $x$-components increase as we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction. Therefore, the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}\\ne 0,$ the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186483377 1071 "Consider the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle F={x}^{2}yz\\,i+{x}^{3}y\\,j+z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\,k.\n$$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186483377 1071 "Consider the vector field $$\n\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{x}^{3}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+z\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$" 13194139