\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,1)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (1) & =4(0)+2 \\\\ 1 & =2\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,6)$ results in true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (6) & =4(1)+2 \\\\ 6 & =6\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line." 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186498590 1071 "![](images/e9/e9892ef4efa3481ad02f7b8e38f1bae1876b196627204521d011656010297281.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=6,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186498590 1071 "![](images/e9/e9892ef4efa3481ad02f7b8e38f1bae1876b196627204521d011656010297281.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=16,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=6,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=4,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186498590 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/b2/b20b2b911c2a417c26fa951d9b28efa296c265df25e9d06b635ef4846267b93b.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =16+6+4 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186498590 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/b2/b20b2b911c2a417c26fa951d9b28efa296c265df25e9d06b635ef4846267b93b.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =16+6+4 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186511457 1071 "![](images/93/938f91220033a2174c011c1f8d65ff61e81e79b8af0ef718411169364c885418.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1-\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)=t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186511457 1071 "![](images/93/938f91220033a2174c011c1f8d65ff61e81e79b8af0ef718411169364c885418.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i+\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186511457 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)=\\mathbf{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}2t]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0]-[-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186511457 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\,\\text{