\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} \\\\ &= {x}^{4}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186532677 1071 "Given that $F(x,y)=x{e}^{y}\\,i+y{x}^{2}\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186532677 1071 "Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x{e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ what is the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}?$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186532677 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}) \\\\  & =2xy-x{e}^{y} \\\\  & =x(2y-{e}^{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186532677 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}) \\\\  & =2xy-x{e}^{y} \\\\  & =x(2y-{e}^{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186534999 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(x+2) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+3 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(y+3) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+2)}^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{36}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186534999 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(x+2) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+3 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(y+3) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+2)}^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{36}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186535012 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186535012 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore