we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186604621 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,{x}^{7}+y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{5}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186604621 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨3x-\\operatorname{cos}y,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{7}+y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{5}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186604621 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00bceda1bdf4f515646b3a1e8476f1eadde721aa52dbc9be9c7e866bcab99d3b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{7}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{5}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,{|}_{0}^{{x}^{5}}\\,dx \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\,dx \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186604621 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00bceda1bdf4f515646b3a1e8476f1eadde721aa52dbc9be9c7e866bcab99d3b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{7}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{5}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{5}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{{x}^{5}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186614572 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}t=1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}t & =1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}t & =\\sqrt{1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t>0$ and $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, substituting $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we obtain\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{\\,1+{x}^{2}\\,}.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y=\\sqrt{\\,1+{x}^{2}\\,}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186614572 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${csc}^{2}⁡t=1+{cot}^{2}⁡t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{csc}^{2}⁡t & =1+{cot}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡t & =\\sqrt{1+{cot}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t>0$ and $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, substituting $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we obtain\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{\\,1+{x}^{2}\\,}.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y=\\sqrt{\\,1+{x}^{2}\\,}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186616135 1074 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(2,4),B(4,2)$ and $C(5,7).$ \n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(2,4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (4) & =(2)+2 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(4,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (2) & =(4)+2 \\\\ 2 & =6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(5,7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (7) & =(5)+2 \\\\ 7 & =7\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line." 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186616135 1074 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(2,4),B(4,2)$ and $C(5,7).$ \n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(2,4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (4) & =(2)+2 \\\\ 4 & =4\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(4,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (2) & =(4)+2 \\\\ 2 & =6\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(5,7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (7) & =(5)+2 \\\\ 7 & =7\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line." 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186616175 1074 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, we can choose $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(3,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/ff/ffd1eea07781890c1dd539e2e36f796d9b18bb4b05b33a903279b88b329f9082.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1.$ \nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =\\frac{3}{4}(0)+1 \\\\ 4 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=4-\\frac{4}{3}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =4-\\frac{4}{3}(0) \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =4-\\frac{4}{3}(3) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=4-\\frac{4}{3}x$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{4}{3}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0) & =\\frac{4}{3}(3) \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{4}{3}x$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=4-\\frac{4}{3}x,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186616175 1074 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, we can choose $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(3,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/ff/ffd1eea07781890c1dd539e2e36f796d9b18bb4b05b33a903279b88b329f9082.png)\n\nNow, we substitut