}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(12-2x)^{2}+(x-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144-48x+4{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-12x+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-60x+180}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-50x+180} & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-60x+180 & =80 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-60x+100 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+20 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-10) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2,$ $x=10.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186622804 1071 "Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\hspace{0.167em}-6,\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\hspace{0.167em}5,\\hspace{0.167em}9⟩.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186622804 1071 "Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ given that $a=⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\,-6,\\,0⟩$ and $b\\times c=⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\,5,\\,9⟩.$\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186622804 1074 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\hspace{0.167em}-6,\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\hspace{0.167em}5,\\hspace{0.167em}9⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{3})(-\\sqrt{3})+(-6)(5)+(0)(9) \\\\  & =3-30+0 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186622804 1074 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\,-6,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\,5,\\,9⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{3})(-\\sqrt{3})+(-6)(5)+(0)(9) \\\\  & =3-30+0 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186622816 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨6,4,6⟩,$ $\\mathbf{q}=⟨5,0,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-1,4,-2⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q}).$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186622816 1071 "Let $p=⟨6,4,6⟩,$ $q=⟨5,0,2⟩$ and $r=⟨-1,4,-2⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (p\\times q).$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186622816 1074 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{r},$ $\\mathbf{p},$ and $\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)(4\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 6)-4(6\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 6)-2(6\\cdot 0-5\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =-8+72+40 \\\\  & =104.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186622816 1074 "Remember that the scalar triple product $r\\cdot (p\\times q)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $r,$ $p,$ and $q.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot (p\\times q) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)(4\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 6)-4(6\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 6)-2(6\\cdot 0-5\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =-8+72+40 \\\\  & =104.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186622827 1071 "Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}),$ if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨7,-2,-3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,7,5⟩.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186622827 1071 "Find $b\\cdot (c\\times a),$ if $b\\times c=⟨7,-2,-3⟩$ and $a=⟨1,7,5⟩.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186622827 1074 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,7,5⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 7+7\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =7-14-15 \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) &=22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186622827 1074 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,7,5⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 7+7\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =7-14-15 \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) &=22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (c\\times a) &=-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186622835 1071 "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})$, if $\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}=⟨-4,6,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,5,-1⟩.$\n\n$\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186622835 1071 "Find $c\\cdot (a\\times b)$, if $c\\times b=⟨-4,6,3⟩$ and $a=⟨2,5,-1⟩.$\n\n$c\\cdot (a\\times b)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186622835 1074 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =⟨2,5,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,6,3⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-4)+5\\cdot 6+(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-8+30-3 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) &=-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186622835 1074 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (c\\times b) & =⟨2,5,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,6,3⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-4)+5\\cdot 6+(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-8+30-3 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b) &=-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186660918 1071 "Given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨0,2,7⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-3,1,4⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨5,-2,0⟩,$ find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186660918 1071 "Given that $a=⟨0,2,7⟩,$ $b=⟨-3,1,4⟩,$ and $c=⟨5,-2,0⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186660918 1074 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-4\\cdot (-2))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\hspace{0.167em}((-3)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\hspace{0.167em}((-3)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+20\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨8,20,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨0,2,7⟩\\cdot ⟨8,20,1⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot 8+2\\cdot 20+7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+40+7 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186660918 1074 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-4\\cdot (-2))\\,i-\\,((-3)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 5)\\,j+\\,((-3)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 5)\\,k \\\\  & =8\\,i+20\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨8,20,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨0,2,7⟩\\cdot ⟨8,20,1⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot 8+2\\cdot 20+7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+40+7 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186670092 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186670092 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{9}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.$" 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186707146 1071 "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - 3 y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 4$ where $x\\ge0,\\ y\\ge0$." 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186707146 1071 "Evaluate $\\iint_D - 3 y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA$, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 4$ where $x\\ge0,\\ y\\ge0$." 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186707148 1071 "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D x + 2 y\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 9$ where $x\\le0,\\ y\\le0$." 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186707148 1071 "Evaluate $\\iint_D x + 2 y\\,dA$, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 9$ where $x\\le0,\\ y\\le0$." 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [17592186707149 1071 "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 4$ where $x\\ge0,\\ y\\le0$." 13194139543369 17592186576411 true] [17592186707149 1071 "Evaluate $\\iint_D - y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA$, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 4$ where $x\\ge0,\\ y\\le0$." 13194139543369 17592186576411 false] [175921867